## STEP 3 — HYPOTHESES

[HYP] Missing client-certificate (mTLS) enforcement on the live Berlin-Group XS2A production API
class: MISCONFIG
asset: xs2a.tech26.de/v1/berlin-group/v1
confidence: 78
reasoning: 11 Berlin-Group route classes (`/accounts`, `/accounts/{id}`, `/balances`, `/transactions`, `/consents`, `/consents/status`, `/consents/{id}`, `/consents/{id}/status`, `/payments/sepa-credit-transfers/{id}`, `.../status`) return `401 len=0` with `x-envoy-upstream-service-time` (app-layer, est 20ms), while a bogus path under the same base returns `404`. The ELB is named `obnium-mtls-live-fra`; Berlin Group mandates an eIDAS QWAC on every TPP request; N26's own support page states the qualified certificate is required to *establish the TLS connection*. `openssl s_client -tls1_3` to xs2a.tech26.de with no client cert completes with `No client certificate CA names sent` and `Verify return code: 0` — the server never requests a certificate, so no client cert is possible to present. The sole gate is a bearer token.
evidence_needed: a QWAC client certificate plus a token issued for a registered TPP, proving the app never binds the certificate CN/organisation to the token's `client_id`; or a token minted with no QWAC at all being accepted on these routes.
verify_steps: `GET /v1/berlin-group/v1/consents/<tpp-A-consent-id> HTTP/2` + `Host: xs2a.tech26.de` + `Authorization: Bearer <tpp-A-token>` (a) with no client certificate, (b) with a QWAC whose CN differs from the token's client_id; compare status and any `WWW-Authenticate` / `ASPSP-SCA-*` headers.
impact: PSD2 strong customer authentication is reduced to bearer-token possession, so any leaked, stale or mis-issued 20-minute token yields full account listing, balances, transaction history, consent state and SEPA payment initiation for the linked PSU; HIGH, conditional on token compromise, no anonymous access established.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

[HYP] Unattended 180-day access to full account data via unbound fallback refresh-token chain
class: AUTH
asset: aisp.tech26.de/oauth2/token
confidence: 70
reasoning: the fallback AISP spec (fallback-aisp.md) makes the access token 15-minute and marked `Storage: NEVER`, but the refresh token is a **180-day rotating chain** renewed by `POST /oauth2/token` with `grant_type=refresh_token`. That refresh call requires only `refresh_token` in the body plus the `device-token` **request header** — and `device_token` is not issued by N26: the doc instructs the TPP to "provide a unique device_token per client/device connection… a valid UUID v4 as per RFC 4122", i.e. the client invents it. `x-tpp-userip` is likewise client-asserted. The route is live (`GET`-family `/api/v2/accounts` → `401`, est app-layer) and `GET /oauth2/token` returns the same 404 as the known-live POST-only `/api/mfa/challenge`, so its reachability is unresolved rather than closed.
evidence_needed: one TPP sandbox refresh token for the fallback tier, plus a second independently-minted session, to test whether a refresh token issued to session A is accepted when replayed with a freshly invented `device-token` and a different `x-tpp-userip`.
verify_steps: baseline `GET /api/v2/accounts HTTP/1.1` + `Host: aisp.tech26.de` + `Authorization: bearer <tpp-A-access>` + `device-token: <uuid-A>` + `x-tpp-userip: 203.0.113.7`; then repeat the same request with `device-token: <random-uuid-B>` and `x-tpp-userip: 198.51.100.9` (both different from the minting session); compare status and body. Halt on any non-test data.
impact: if `device-token` is not bound server-side to the session, one leaked refresh token yields 180 days of unattended, un-rate-limited access to every account's IBAN, balance, product, owner name and full transaction history including counterparty IBANs and partner names; HIGH.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

[HYP] Cross-tenant BOLA on the fallback PIS/AIS interface with client-asserted anti-fraud binding
class: IDOR
asset: pisp.tech26.de/api/openbanking/fallback/{sepa-ct,sepa-instant,so}/{paymentId}/status and aisp.tech26.de/api/fallback/accounts/{accountId}/transactions
confidence: 68
reasoning: the status routes are live and 401-gated (`sepa-ct/{uuid}/status` → `401`, `so/{uuid}/status` → `401`) and each documented request carries only `device-token` + `x-tpp-userip` — no `Authorization` line in the vendor example (I confirmed the server does enforce authz regardless, so the doc example is an omission, not a flaw). `{accountId}` and `{paymentId}` are attacker-suppliable UUIDs. N26 documents that its entire anti-fraud layer for this interface keys off `x-tpp-userip`: *"providing this IP address guarantees us to identify proper user calls and to protect the integrity of our services… If a TPP doesn't provide an end-user's IP address with API requests requiring IP specification, or tries to manipulate or obfuscate IP addresses, such cases will be treated in accordance to our established security policies."* That control is a plain client-supplied header on the same request.
evidence_needed: two isolated sandbox TPP sessions, stable synthetic `accountId`/`paymentId` values per session, and object-bearing responses.
verify_steps: `GET /api/openbanking/fallback/sepa-ct/<tpp-B-payment-id>/status HTTP/1.1` + `Host: pisp.tech26.de` + `Authorization: bearer <tpp-A-access>` + `device-token: <uuid-A>` + `x-tpp-userip: <tpp-B-user's real IP>`; mirror with token B; same pattern on `GET /api/fallback/accounts/<tpp-B-accountId>/transactions?from=0&to=1` at aisp.tech26.de. Halt on any non-test data.
impact: cross-tenant disclosure of payment execution state, rejection reasons, and full account transaction history with counterparty identifiers; additionally, spoofing `x-tpp-userip` with the victim's real IP neutralises the documented fraud controls, so a stolen fallback token is used without tripping IP-anomaly detection; CRITICAL conditional on BOLA, no anonymous access established.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

## STEP 4 — SELF-CRITIQUE

[PARKED] vhost route confusion / token-audience bypass on the shared `obnium-mtls-live-fra` ELB: tested and **refuted** — route tables are strictly disjoint per Host (aisp serves only `/api/v2/accounts`, pisp only the fallback payment routes, xs2a only `/v1/berlin-group/v1/*`; every cross-cell returns `404`, bogus-path control `/api/openbanking/fallback/sepa-ct-x/status` → `404`). This was my own hypothesis, generated from the shared-ELB discovery, and it does not hold.
[PARKED] unauthenticated payment-status read via missing `Authorization` header: refuted — `device-token` + `x-tpp-userip` with no `Authorization` returned `401` on all three status routes. The vendor doc example omitting the header is a documentation defect, not an exploitable gap; reporting it would be a descriptive-error/no-POC rejection.
