[PARKED] unauthenticated payment-status read via missing `Authorization` header: refuted — `device-token` + `x-tpp-userip` with no `Authorization` returned `401` on all three status routes. The vendor doc example omitting the header is a documentation defect, not an exploitable gap; reporting it would be a descriptive-error/no-POC rejection.
[PARKED] `api.tech26.de` legacy BOLA (92) and `pay.n26.com` Stripe-forwarded BOLA (85): both remain **valid and undischarged** AUTH_HELPED items, retained below the top-3 cut only because this cycle's entire new evidence concerns the fallback/XS2A tier. Neither is invalidated.
[FINAL] 1. xs2a.tech26.de missing mTLS with vendor-documented control (78 — the only one demonstrable without any credential); 2. aisp.tech26.de unbound 180-day refresh chain (70); 3. fallback-tier BOLA with client-asserted `x-tpp-userip` (68).
[NEXT] PROBE: `POST /oauth2/token` on `pisp.tech26.de` with `Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, `device-token: <fresh uuid4>`, body `refresh_token=00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000000&grant_type=refresh_token` — a syntactically valid but fabricated token, no username/password, so no login attempt and no username enumeration. This settles the one open question my own LEARN flags as unsound: whether the fallback password/refresh grant is anonymously reachable, which GET-404 cannot answer. Requires sign-off because it is method-correct POST; not run under this cycle's GET/HEAD-only constraint.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ {xs2a,aisp,pisp}.tech26.de: all three share ELB `obnium-mtls-live-fra-328671153` and none ever issues a TLS `CertificateRequest` (`No client certificate CA names sent`), despite N26's public support page stating aisp/pisp "Access to the above URL and establishing the TLS connection requires implementation of a qualified certificate". Second independent instance of the 09-25 rule — a generic wordlist probed before the real route table produces systematic false negatives; the correct enumeration source is the vendor's own `psd2-tpp-docs`, and it reclassified aisp/pisp from "unmapped" to a live money-movement surface.
[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ app.n26.com `/open-banking/{aisp,cbpii}`: `state` is passed through as an opaque query param into the server-rebuilt `/login?...&redirect=` chain, and `redirect` is server-constructed from the matched route path (09-26 finding holds) — no open redirect and no observable state-binding failure; auth-code theft on the new Redirect SCA mode is AUTH_HELPED at best, not reportable.
[LEARN] REJECTED AUTH @ pisp.tech26.de + aisp.tech26.de: the "the TPP must not use access/refresh tokens on other base URLs" and "PISP tokens cannot be used for AISP flows" audience-separation controls **are enforced at the routing layer** — the three route tables are strictly disjoint per Host, so a token minted on one host has no route to reach on another. Closes the cross-deployment audience-confusion item parked at 55 last cycle.
[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ xs2a.tech26.de `/oauth2/token`: my own 09-26 entry "token-minting surface is not anonymously reachable" is retracted as unsound — `GET /api/mfa/challenge` (documented POST-only, confirmed live) returns a byte-identical 404 on this edge, so GET-404 cannot distinguish absent from POST-only. Generalised rule: on this program, a 404 on a GET is only evidence of route absence for routes documented as GET; for documented-POST routes it is evidence of nothing.
[RISK] N26 Bank AG: 90 — the open-banking tier is materially worse than the KB reflected. Two previously-"unmapped" hosts are in fact the **PSD2 contingency/fallback interface with live payment initiation** (`/api/openbanking/fallback/sepa-ct`, `.../sepa-instant`, `/api/transactions/so`) sitting on the same load balancer as the dedicated Berlin-Group API, across which the one documented transport control — a qualified client certificate required to establish the TLS connection — is not enforced on any of the three hostnames, with an internet client completing TLS 1.3 and reaching the app unauthenticated at the transport layer. The fallback design compounds it: a 180-day rotating refresh-token chain on a client-invented `device-token`, with the documented anti-fraud controls keyed to a client-asserted `x-tpp-userip` header. Still zero validated findings, zero anonymous data access, and every read/write path remains bearer-gated; the control-gap itself is the part provable today.
