```
[LEARN] ACCEPTED OATH @ xs2a.tech26.de: the authorization endpoint's pre-auth gate is a single exact, case-sensitive, single-token `scope` match that validates the scope *value* while performing no `client_id` validation at all — a fabricated `client_id=zzz` reaches the 401 auth gate identically to the published `PSDDE-BAFIN-000001`, and the real client_id with an unrecognised scope returns 404. This refutes the client_id-enumeration reading of the 09-26 404/401 split and means the published Berlin-Group client_ids are unverified against the live AS.
[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ xs2a.tech26.de `/sandbox/psu-interaction/generate-auth-code/{id}`: my 09-26 "not present on the production host" rested on a param-less GET, which my own rule says proves nothing for a documented-POST route. Re-tested with 7 GET shapes plus OPTIONS — and OPTIONS is proven method-agnostic on this edge (the GET-only control answers 401, not 405) — the helper returns 404 identically to a bogus sibling while the co-located live sandbox Berlin-Group route returns 401. The credential-free auth-code-mint escalation is now materially weakened; only a POST would fully close it, and that is outside the method envelope.
[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ xs2a.tech26.de forwarding-header tier selection: `X-Forwarded-Host`, `X-Forwarded-Prefix`, `X-Original-URL`, `X-Rewrite-URL` and `X-Forwarded-Proto` produce no routing or gate change on either the authorize plane or the Berlin-Group API — all 8 variants hold 401/0/istio-envoy. The gateway does not route on client-supplied forwarding headers, so sandbox/live separation is not defeatable this way and the audience-confusion hypothesis loses this axis.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ process: the discriminator I asserted two steps into this cycle was wrong and only a deliberate bogus-value control caught it. On any endpoint that returns two distinct error codes, the first task is to identify which *input dimension* selects between them — not to assume the dimension that is salient in the vendor documentation. Here the salient dimension (`client_id`, prominent in the Postman collection) was the wrong one and the actual dimension (`scope`) was a single query parameter. Generalised: a negative conclusion built on an assumed error-code discriminator is worth nothing until a positive and a negative control are run against that same dimension.
```

