[STEP 5 — NEXT ACTION]

[NEXT] PROBE: determine whether the ALB filter is scoped to the authorize path or is a host-wide query-value rule — `for q in 'x=https://127.0.0.1/' 'x=//127.0.0.1/' 'x=foo'; do printf '%-24s ' "$q"; curl -G -s -o /dev/null -m 20 -w '%{http_code} len=%{size_download}\n' --data-urlencode "${q%%=*}=${q#*=}" "https://xs2a.tech26.de/v1/berlin-group/v1/accounts"; sleep 1.2; done` with `x=https://127.0.0.1/` → 403 would prove the blocklist is a host-wide generic WAF rule rather than a deliberate redirect_uri control, which changes the report framing from "bypassed security control" to "unintended host-wide behaviour + no application-layer control" and states the affected scope accurately. Read-only, GET only, 3 requests + 1 control.

[STEP 6 — LEARNING]

[LEARN] ACCEPTED OATH @ xs2a.tech26.de: the redirect_uri blocklist **requires a `scheme://` literal to engage and performs no URI parsing whatsoever**. Proof that it is a non-parsing substring matcher rather than a host-aware filter, from two requests the filter's own behaviour made decisive: `https://127.0.0.1@evil.example/` → **403** although the effective host is a public domain, and `https://evil.example@127.0.0.1/` → **401** although the effective host is loopback. Scheme-relative `//169.254.169.254/`, `//10.0.0.1/`, `//192.168.0.1/`, `//172.16.0.1/`, `//[::1]/`, `//0x7f000001/`, `//127.0.0.1/`, `//localtest.me/`, `/127.0.0.1/` and bare `0x7f.1/` all reach the AS (401 `istio-envoy`, CL=0) where the same targets with `https://` are cut at the ALB (403 `awselb/2.0`, 118B nginx). Header classes reconfirmed; blocklist present and equally bypassable on `/sandbox/oauth2/authorize`. Generalised rule: **when a blocklist's bypasses cluster around *notation* rather than *syntax position*, the cheapest discriminator is a value with a benign effective host that merely contains a blocked token** — a naive regex blocks it, a real URI parser allows it. That single request separates "narrow parser bug" from "no parser at all" and would have saved me a cycle: I spent 09-26 characterising `0x7f.1` as an integer-parser defect, and that framing was wrong — `0x7f.1` is just one of 10+ hosts reachable by a defect that has nothing to do with integer parsing. Same error family as my earlier presence-vs-value mistake (on `/oauth2/token`, `generate-auth-code`, scope), now generalised into a reusable check: **before characterising *how* a filter parses, prove *whether* it parses.**
[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ process: a control discovered on one tier must be re-run on every sibling tier before the control is described as a program-level property. The redirect_uri blocklist was mapped on the live authorize plane over three cycles and I recorded it as a property of the host; testing `/sandbox/oauth2/authorize` for the first time this cycle showed the same ALB rule with the same bypass. Two of my FINAL items concern this host and I had never cross-checked a single control between its live and sandbox authorization planes. Rule: for every accepted control-failure finding, the NEXT action must include the same request against each co-tenant/sibling tier on the same edge, before the finding is written up as scoped.

[STEP 7 — RISK]

[RISK] N26 Bank AG: 94 — up from 93. The headline is unchanged and still the provable one: the production Berlin-Group XS2A API answers an anonymous internet client over an `obnium-mtls-live-fra` ALB that never issues a TLS CertificateRequest, against Berlin Group's eIDAS QWAC mandate and N26's own published statement that a qualified certificate is required. Reconfirmed live this cycle on two hosts sharing that ELB, so the only credential-free high-severity item remains undischarged. The composition improved materially: the redirect_uri control on the same host's authorization plane is now fully characterised as a substring regex that requires a `scheme://` literal and does no URI parsing, bypassable with a single character-class change to reach `//169.254.169.254/`, RFC1918, IPv6 and IPv4 loopback, on the live *and* sandbox authorize planes, with zero application-layer validation behind it and no client_id validation either. The AS can therefore be steered into the PSU's own loopback and internal-network position from a trusted N26 origin. I also corrected two of my own prior claims — the mixed-hex "integer parser" mechanism (wrong: the defect is structural and 10x broader) and the earlier absence of any sandbox-plane control testing — both of which had been carried forward at fixed confidence without re-derivation. Still zero validated findings, zero anonymous data access, every response 0 bytes; the exploit half of the redirect chain and both BOLA items remain AUTH_HELPED. Outstanding coordinator inputs, unchanged and both now blocking the two highest-value items: a TPP/PSU sandbox identity for xs2a.tech26.de, and paired-device grant tokens for api.tech26.de.
