## STEP 6 — LEARNING

- **[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ xs2a.tech26.de: the absent mTLS check is the entire identity control, not a missing second factor — established from the vendor's own documentation, which is what turned a conf-94 observation into a severity claim.** `doc/redirect/dedicated-aisp.md` L78-81: *"POST /oauth2/token over an mTLS connection presenting its eIDAS QWAC certificate — there is no browser-based PSU login, no authorization code, and no token exchange step. N26 derives the TPP's `client_id` from the certificate."* I had been scoring this as "the mandated transport-layer client authentication is absent, so the control degrades to a single bearer token" — defence-in-depth language. The document says the certificate is how the AS learns *who the caller is*; there is no other identification channel in the design. Generalised rule: **when a mandatory transport control is absent, the vendor's own description of what that control *is for* determines the severity, not the control's name in the spec.** A missing "mTLS" and a missing "password" can be the same finding.
- **[LEARN] REJECTED OATH @ xs2a.tech26.de `/oauth2/authorize`: my 09-26 and 09-27 authorize-plane findings measured a plane the vendor states is not used by this deployment.** Two independent refutations, both needed. First, the 403 is not a redirect_uri control: it is param-name-agnostic (`?x=` ≡ `?redirect_uri=`), path-agnostic (fires identically on the Berlin-Group API path), host-agnostic (118-byte body byte-identical, `sha256 58bf2215b395dcac74c009aa98701854`, on the unrelated `app.n26.com` ALB), and remote hosts are never blocked — it is a generic ALB anti-SSRF pattern rule matching `<scheme>://<private-host>`, evidenced by `foo://127.0.0.1` → 403 while bare `127.0.0.1` → 401. Second, and decisive, the redirect docs state the flow uses a cert-only `client_credentials` grant with "no authorization code, and no token exchange step", so `client_id` and `redirect_uri` are not client-supplied inputs in this design at all. I had built a three-cycle evidence chain — including a 10x correction and a self-derived rule — on top of a generic WAF default page. **Generalised rule: before characterising what a security control does, prove the input it inspects is an input the application accepts.** A filter that fires identically on a parameter the application never reads, on a path it does not serve, on a host that does not host the flow, is a provider rule, and every bypass of it is a finding about the provider. Corollaries that did hold up: the "prove whether it parses, before characterising how it parses" check from 09-27 was correct and I should have applied its stronger sibling first; and a control's coverage must be tested against the channel the value actually travels on — the WAF inspects query values only, while the real redirect travels in a request header and is provably uncovered (`TPP-Redirect-URI: https://127.0.0.1/` → 401, indistinguishable from baseline, same value as `?x=` → 403).

## STEP 7 — RISK

**[RISK] N26 Bank AG: 95 — up from 94. The headline is unchanged and still the credential-free one: the production Berlin-Group XS2A API answers an anonymous internet client over an `obnium-mtls-live-fra` ALB that never issues a TLS CertificateRequest, against Berlin Group's eIDAS QWAC mandate and N26's own published statement that a qualified certificate is required. Reconfirmed live this cycle on two hosts sharing that ELB. What changed is that the finding is no longer correctly scored. My prior framing treated the missing certificate as a lost second factor behind a bearer token; the vendor's redirect documentation states that N26 derives the TPP's `client_id` from the certificate and that the token is minted by a cert-only `client_credentials` grant with no other identification channel. The absent control is therefore the identity itself, not a layer above it, and the composed chain is new: `TPP-Redirect-URI` is a client-supplied post-authentication redirect target whose only documented validation is "malformed", and the host's single edge control provably does not inspect request headers at all, so any caller who can create a consent can steer an authenticated PSU to an arbitrary host from a trusted `app.n26.com` origin with the TPP's `state` echoed. I also retired my own top-ranked finding from the previous cycle after refuting it on two independent grounds, which is a real reduction in claimed severity and the reason the score moved only one point rather than spiking on the new chain. Still zero validated findings, zero anonymous data access, every response 0 bytes. Outstanding coordinator inputs, unchanged and now blocking the only reportable chain in the program: a TPP/PSU sandbox identity with a QWAC for xs2a.tech26.de, and paired-device grant tokens for api.tech26.de.
