## STEP 6 — LEARNING

**[LEARN] ACCEPTED OATH @ xs2a.tech26.de + estate-wide**: the redirect_uri blocklist is **not a redirect_uri control and not host-scoped** — it is one generic AWS ALB WAF rule matching a literal `://` + blocked-host substring, deployed across `api`, `beta-api`, `sapi`, `aisp`, `pisp`, `xs2a`, `app.n26.com` and `support.n26.com` (identical 403/118 `awselb/2.0`), exempt only on the `fpt.tech26.de` fixed-response stub. Proven by `a://127.0.0.1/` and `xyz://127.0.0.1/` → 403 (no scheme semantics ⇒ no parsing) plus a fire on a non-OAuth path under an arbitrary param name `x`. The consequence is stronger than the original claim, not weaker: **the application layer has no redirect_uri validation whatsoever, and the edge filter that appeared to cover it does not** — it is blind to request headers, which is exactly where the vendor's own corpus puts `TPP-Redirect-URI`. Generalised rule, extending my 09-27 "prove *whether* it parses": **when a control is mapped on one service, prove its trigger surface too** — path vs query vs header vs body. A control that only covers query strings may be invisible to the parameter that actually matters, and an identical-looking 403 on a completely unrelated path is the cheapest proof that you have been attributing a generic edge rule to a specific application control.

**[LEARN] REJECTED OTHER @ xs2a.tech26.de filter**: uppercase scheme is **not** a bypass — `HTTPS://127.0.0.1/` → 403. The matcher is case-insensitive, so the case-variation bypass that is standard in hand-rolled WAF regexes is closed here. Recorded because it is the first bypass candidate I tested and eliminated, and the negative materially bounds the filter's description.

## STEP 7 — RISK

**[RISK] N26 Bank AG: 94** — unchanged, and deliberately not moved. The headline is untouched and still the provable one: the production Berlin-Group XS2A API answers an anonymous internet client over an `obnium-mtls-live-fra` ELB that never issues a TLS CertificateRequest, against Berlin Group's eIDAS QWAC mandate and N26's own corpus statement that the certificate *is* the TPP identity — so the gap is the entire control, not a missing second factor. This cycle made the redirect composition substantially more accurate but not more severe, and one component of it was **removed**: the 403 was never a redirect_uri security control, so "bypassed a dedicated control" was an over-claim I was carrying, and retracting it is why the score did not rise despite a 10x-wider filter scope and the new header-blindness result. The scope genuinely grew — the same blind filter now covers the customer-facing `app.n26.com` / `support.n26.com` web tier as well as the whole banking estate — and the AS-side validation gap is now unambiguous rather than inferred. Still zero validated findings, zero anonymous data access, every response body 0 bytes; the exploit half of the redirect chain and both BOLA items remain AUTH_HELPED. Outstanding coordinator inputs, both blocking the two highest-value items: a TPP/PSU sandbox identity for xs2a.tech26.de, and paired-device grant tokens for api.tech26.de.
