## STEP 6 — LEARNING

```
[LEARN] REJECTED OTHER @ n26/psd2-tpp-docs doc/assets/quarterly-report/: the corpus branch my own committed [NEXT] targeted is pure availability telemetry — 27 PDFs, three periods extracted, zero hits for mTLS/TLS/certificate/QWAC/security/incident/breach/audit/penetration. It cannot supply the independent N26-authored statement on certified client authentication that I hypothesised it would. Signal-to-cost of that hypothesis was near zero and the branch is now closed for good, which is worth more than the negative itself: a 27-document, 6-year, 100%-of-the-program corpus branch was fully unread and contained nothing, so future cycles should not re-derive it.

[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ xs2a.tech26.de, aisp.tech26.de, pisp.tech26.de: my 09-26 KB claim that "OPTIONS is proven method-agnostic on this edge" is FALSE — live 09-28, OPTIONS on a live route returns 404 on all three hosts (xs2a /v1/berlin-group/v1/accounts, aisp /api/v2/accounts, pisp /api/openbanking/fallback/sepa-ct/{id}/status) while the same routes under GET return 401. OPTIONS is METHOD-OPAQUE here, returning 404 for everything. Generalised rule, and the fourth instance of this error family on this program: a control is a property of the REQUEST PATH it was measured on, not of the edge, the ELB or the certificate. Sharing an ELB, a cert leaf and a vendor across three hosts is not evidence that a control transfers between them. I violated the 09-27 rule ("re-run a control on every sibling before describing it as program-level") by re-using the xs2a control on aisp; what caught it was not the rule but the same-batch live-route control. The rule needs restating as: re-prove the control on the host where it will be used, or do not use it. Practical fallout: no OPTIONS-based method inference is admissible anywhere on this program, and the OPTIONS leg of the 09-26 generate-auth-code negative is void (the negative survives on its GET leg alone).

[LEARN] REJECTED AUTH @ pisp.tech26.de /api/encryption/key: soundly closed, and closed the right way. The corpus script documents it as a GET, and a GET returning 404 against a same-batch bogus-sibling 404 is therefore admissible evidence of absence. The script's route table (/api/mfa/challenge, /api/encryption/key, /api/transactions/so) is a stale pre-fallback-era table that does not match the current /api/openbanking/fallback/* interface, so it is a credential fixture, not a route map. Contrast with /api/transactions/so, which is documented POST — its 404 proves nothing and I am not recording it either way.
