## Summary

**Headline: I retracted my own #2-ranked, 93-confidence finding.**

**What I tested.** The strongest untested axis on the `redirect_uri` blocklist I had accepted on 09-27/09-28. I ran 23 paced GETs, no credentials, zero mutations, every application-layer body 0 bytes.

**The error.** I had measured that the blocklist is bypassable (hex notation, scheme-relative `//`, userinfo confusion) and concluded it was the sole `redirect_uri` control. I never checked **which parameter** the filter was scoped to. One request settled it: an invented, semantically meaningless `foo=https://127.0.0.1/` also returns `403 awselb/2.0` — identical cut. Same for `state` and `request_uri`. Meanwhile `127.0.0.1` in the **path** passes (404/401 `istio-envoy`).

So it is a **generic query-string WAF blocklist** on the shared ELB, not an OAuth control. There was never a `redirect_uri` control to bypass, therefore no authorization-code-theft finding. The 09-28 sub-observation (AS does no pre-auth `redirect_uri` *value* validation) survives on its own measurements, but as a missing-feature note, not a bypass.

**Why I logged it as the sharpest process lesson.** I burned two cycles and ~25 requests characterising *how* the filter parses — hex, octal, port, scheme case, host case — while the question that mattered was *what it is scoped to*. Each bypass felt like progress and each was individually reproducible; none were evidence about OAuth. The corrective is now a reusable rule: before writing "control X bypasses control Y", prove the filter is scoped to Y.

**Incidental value.** The filter's true shape is documented precisely (case-insensitive, port/short-form tolerant, matches only at authority start, edge-wide across xs2a+pisp). That will stop future probes from reading a `403` as "this route is blocked" when it is only the WAF.

**Unchanged finals:** xs2a QWAC unenforced (92) · pisp credential-blind resource plane (86) · api/beta-api/sapi unfulfilled deprecation (86).

**Two flags for you:**
1. **Feeder is down** — `Unexpected server error` on consecutive runs (`err_6b3f634b`, `err_ed472fed`); `probe-results.md` was frozen since 09-28 before I appended. Nothing is currently reaching the coordinator, which is now a bigger engagement risk than any individual finding.
2. **Next probe queued** — SNI-bounding test (4 GETs) to establish whether xs2a is reachable without negotiating a QWAC. Still needs no approval.

Cheapest unblock remains the one-line approval for the single `POST /oauth2/token`.
