670:- 2026-09-27 ACCEPTED OATH @ xs2a.tech26.de: the redirect_uri blocklist **requires a `scheme://` literal to engage and performs no URI parsing whatsoever**. Proof that it is a non-parsing substring matcher rather than a host-aware filter, from two requests the filter's own behaviour made decisive: `https://127.0.0.1@evil.example/` → **403** although the effective host is a public domain, and `https://evil.example@127.0.0.1/` → **401** although the effective host is loopback. Scheme-relative `//169.254.169.254/`, `//10.0.0.1/`, `//192.168.0.1/`, `//172.16.0.1/`, `//[::1]/`, `//0x7f000001/`, `//127.0.0.1/`, `//localtest.me/`, `/127.0.0.1/` and bare `0x7f.1/` all reach the AS (401 `istio-envoy`, CL=0) where the same targets with `https://` are cut at the ALB (403 `awselb/2.0`, 118B nginx). Header classes reconfirmed; blocklist present and equally bypassable on `/sandbox/oauth2/authorize`. Generalised rule: **when a blocklist's bypasses cluster around *notation* rather than *syntax position*, the cheapest discriminator is a value with a benign effective host that merely contains a blocked token** — a naive regex blocks it, a real URI parser allows it. That single request separates "narrow parser bug" from "no parser at all" and would have saved me a cycle: I spent 09-26 characterising `0x7f.1` as an integer-parser defect, and that framing was wrong — `0x7f.1` is just one of 10+ hosts reachable by a defect that has nothing to do with integer parsing. Same error family as my earlier presence-vs-value mistake (on `/oauth2/token`, `generate-auth-code`, scope), now generalised into a reusable check: **before characterising *how* a filter parses, prove *whether* it parses.**
