## DELTA

**1. The redirect headers are documented on the PRODUCTION base URL, not only sandbox** — this was the open question from the last `[NEXT]` and it is now closed.

`doc/redirect/dedicated-{aisp,pisp,cbpii}.md` all state Base URL `https://xs2a.tech26.de`, Sandbox URL `https://xs2a.tech26.de/sandbox`. The `TPP-Redirect-Preferred` / `TPP-Redirect-URI` / `TPP-State` table appears under the production-base-URL section for consent creation (`POST /v1/berlin-group/v1/consents` for AISP/PISP, `POST /v1/berlin-group/v1/consents/confirmation-of-funds` for CBPII) and, for PISP, additionally on `POST /v1/berlin-group/v1/payments/{payment-product}`.

- **DOC-CLAIM (not observed):** malformed/missing `TPP-Redirect-URI` with `TPP-Redirect-Preferred=true` → `400 FORMAT_ERROR`.
- **DOC-CLAIM (not observed):** N26 redirects the PSU to `TPP-Redirect-URI?state=<TPP-State>`, or `?error=access_denied&state=...` on failure. `TPP-State` is reflected verbatim into the callback URL.
- **No redirect-URI allowlist or per-client registration is documented anywhere in the corpus.** Absence of documentation is not evidence of absence in the implementation — logged as such, not as a finding.
- **MITIGATOR (decisive):** every consent and payment route requires a valid eIDAS **QWAC** certificate and `POST /oauth2/token` derives `client_id` from the certificate. The header is unreachable for an anonymous researcher. Severity stays MEDIUM-with-caveat, not escalated.

**2. New route-existence control — and a correction that removes a claim I had been leaning on.**

I probed four payment paths, then fabricated siblings. The result invalidates leaf-level enumeration on this host:

| Probe (`xs2a.tech26.de`) | Result |
|---|---|
| `/v1/berlin-group/v1/payments/sepa-credit-transfers` | `401|0` |
| `/v1/berlin-group/v1/payments/instant-sepa-credit-transfers` | `401|0` |
| `/v1/berlin-group/v1/payments/single-irecurring-transfers` | `401\|0` |
| **`/v1/berlin-group/v1/payments/zzqnotreal-not-a-product`** (control) | **`401|0`** |
| `/v1/berlin-group/v1/periodic-payments/zzqnotreal-not-a-product` (control) | `401|0` |
| `/v1/berlin-group/v1/zzqnotreal-not-a-route` | `404|0` |
| `/v1/berlin-group/v1/funds-confirmations` | `404\|0` |

A **fabricated** payment product returns the same `401|0` as the two real ones. So `401` under `/payments/*` and `/periodic-payments/*` is a **route-group gate**, not proof the leaf exists. `single-irecurring-transfers` is **not** a confirmed route — it appears nowhere in the corpus. `/v1/berlin-group/v1/funds-confirmations` is a genuine `404`, consistent with the docs, where funds confirmation is performed through the consent rather than a separate collection path.

This is the same `api.tech26.de` prefix-collapse lesson reproduced on a second host. It also means the 1ms-bogus vs 8–11ms-app timing signature did **not** reproduce this cycle (all reads 0.41–0.46s, overlapping); I am treating that timing differential as unreliable and am no longer using it to license route claims.

**3. The ALB query-filter is confirmed on all four hosts and is fully path-agnostic.**

Every one of these returned `403|118 awselb` with the `foo=https://127.0.0.1@evil.example/` query string, including paths that do not exist and the site root:

`xs2a.tech26.de`, `aisp.tech26.de`, `pisp.tech26.de`, `api.tech26.de` — all `403|118` with the payload, all `404|0` or `401|0` without it.

Per the 09-27 rule this was re-probed per host rather than transferred from the prior cycle. The filter fires on `/`, on `/zzqnotreal-nothing`, on `/oauth2/token`, and on every Berlin-Group prefix. It is a shared edge control, query-string-scoped, and blind to headers — consistent with the `TPP-Redirect-URI` header passing straight through.

