## STEP 7 — RISK

```
[RISK] n26: 41 — The finding is real and the evidence discipline held: field-scoping control, determinism loop, and a closed XSS axis rather than an assumed one, on 12 anonymous GETs with the form never submitted. What keeps this at 41 rather than 70 is one specific unknown — Relay's `__successRedirect` is executed server-side per spec, but I have not observed the navigation, and the severity split (MEDIUM phishing vs HIGH code theft) turns entirely on whether the post-login continuation appends `code=`/`state=`/session material. My prior cycle's retraction on `/open-banking*` is the cautionary half: the same parameter name behaved in the opposite direction one path away, so the decisive artifact is N26's own bundle, not another status code. Residual program risk is unchanged elsewhere — xs2a/api write-plane and BOLA items stay credential-blocked, and no customer data was touched at any point this cycle.
```
## Objective
- Continue the N26 bug-bounty POC cycle (`phase: POC`, `target: api`) and produce the required 8-step analyst output using passive, read-only evidence.
- Test whether `app.n26.com/login?redirect=<external URL>` reflects or consumes an attacker-controlled destination, separately from the already-rejected `/open-banking` redirect behavior.

## Important Details
- Stay within N26-owned in-scope assets; live probes must be passive-first, GET/HEAD/OPTIONS only, ≤1 rps, and must not touch customer data or mutate state.
- For `app.n26.com`, use a browser User-Agent, `Accept-Encoding: identity`, and 6-second spacing; known limit is `x-ratelimit-limit: 60`.
- Fix transfer encoding and repeat identical requests before treating response differences as discriminators.
- `/open-banking` direct open redirect is rejected: server-built `redirect` replaces attacker input and reflected `state` is double-encoded.
- Direct `/login?redirect=` is a distinct channel and remains unresolved; reflection alone does not prove navigation or an exploitable open redirect.
- Existing parked leads: `TPP-Redirect-*` header filtering requires authentication; CBPII cross-role consent testing needs a dedicated token; `POST /oauth2/token` write-plane testing is `HUMAN_ONLY`.

