submit: "SUCCESS"===status
  ? (typeof onSuccess==="function" ? onSuccess(...) : this.props.history.push(this.getSuccessURL()))
  : (typeof onFailure==="function" ? onFailure(s) : /* no navigation */)
```

**RETACTED: "Relay performs a server-side 302 to `__successRedirect`."** False. The form's `onSubmit` intercepts and mutates via Relay; the native POST never happens when JS runs. And the login page passes `onSuccess`/`onFailure` handlers (`lp.js`: `onSuccess:function(e){…LOGIN_CREDENTIALS_SUCCESS…}, successURL:e.redirectURL, failureURL:e.redirectURL`), so the `history.push(successURL)` branch and the failure navigation are **never reached**. The hidden fields are vestigial progressive-enhancement markup.

## The real sink — and it is not the query param

```js
// lp.js — login_open_banking_redirection component
try { if (n) { var e = new URL(n);
      if (/^(https?):$/.test(e.protocol)) return void (window.location.href = e.toString()) } }
catch(t){} u("login_open_banking_redirection_failed")
```

Genuine cross-origin `window.location.href` on any http(s) URL, no host allowlist. But its input is not the query param — the reducer sets it from the **server's response**:

```js
case m: return n.response ? {…e, redirectUrl: n.response.redirectUrl, loginScreen: l.kv.OPEN_BANKING_REDIRECTION} : e
```

And the same bundle contains a *contradicting* treatment of the same param:

```js
new URL(r, "https://n26.com").pathname   // strips scheme+host — safe path
```

So N26 sanitizes `redirect` to a pathname in one place and navigates to the raw value in another.

## Revised verdict

| | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| Mechanism | server 302 per Relay spec | SSR-echoed hidden input (JS-dead) + `window.location.href` driven by `response.redirectUrl` |
| Confidence | 75 | **55** |
| Class | OATH | OTHER (CWE-601 exposure, unproven reachability) |
| Impact | MEDIUM, HIGH if params appended | **LOW–MEDIUM, unconfirmed** |

What stays proven: the `redirect` param is reflected unvalidated into both SSR hidden inputs on the real login origin, `__failureRedirect` included — field-scoped (`?zzz=` → baseline-identical), size-deterministic (3/3 at 316306B), XSS axis closed, edge 403s markup/`javascript:`. What is now **unproven**: whether the server ever propagates the attacker's `redirect` into `response.redirectUrl`. That one fact is the whole finding.

This also partially rehabilitates the KB line I retracted last block — the server *does* rebuild/validate the response-side redirect; what I found is the SSR echo, not a live sink. My retraction was itself an over-generalization in the other direction, and I should not have shipped it at confidence.

